      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Once a person turns 65 or has received federal disability benefts for 24 months, he becomes \u201centitled\u201d to benefts under Part A of Medicare."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1312",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6cd983751b20af09cd946e2c166d6d1f8e754628d8fe4849801e0125ca799655",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6cd983751b20-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "certiorari to the united states court of appeals for the ninth circuit"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1312",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6cd983751b20af09cd946e2c166d6d1f8e754628d8fe4849801e0125ca799655",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6cd983751b20-0000-0006",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The combined percentage must usually equal or exceed 15% for a hospital to get an adjustment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Empire Health Foundation",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1312",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6cd983751b20af09cd946e2c166d6d1f8e754628d8fe4849801e0125ca799655",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6cd983751b20-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondent Empire Health Foundation challenged the regulation as inconsistent with the statutory fraction descriptions, and the Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit agreed. See Empire Health Foundation v. Azar, 958 F. 3d 873 (2020)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1312",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6cd983751b20af09cd946e2c166d6d1f8e754628d8fe4849801e0125ca799655",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6cd983751b20-0001-0002",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The structure of the relevant statutory provisions reinforces our conclusion that \u201centitled to [Part A] benefts\u201d means qualifying for those benefts, and nothing more."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Empire",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1312",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6cd983751b20af09cd946e2c166d6d1f8e754628d8fe4849801e0125ca799655",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6cd983751b20-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Empire responds by asserting that any low-income person excluded from the Medicare fraction (say, because of exhaustion of benefts) would get counted instead in the Medicaid fraction. See Brief for Respondent 15\u201316, 50\u201351."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Empire",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1312",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6cd983751b20af09cd946e2c166d6d1f8e754628d8fe4849801e0125ca799655",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6cd983751b20-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "According to Empire's ultimate argument, that phrase is what converts the ordinary statutory meaning of \u201centitled to benefts\u201d (i. e., qualifying for Medicare) to a special meaning (i. e., actually receiving payments)."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
